00:10:18.840 --> 00:10:21.620
Two robotics stories landed close together.

00:10:21.740 --> 00:10:23.900
and they make more sense side by side.

00:10:24.540 --> 00:10:25.480
First, Toyota.

00:10:26.160 --> 00:10:30.320
In September, Reuters reported that Toyota told investors its group may need

00:10:30.320 --> 00:10:33.440
around 400,000 robots to automate its factories.

00:10:34.200 --> 00:10:40.880
It estimated the cost at about 1 trillion yen, or $6.4 billion, a year from 2028.

00:10:41.420 --> 00:10:44.780
That covers Toyota, its group companies, and major suppliers.

00:10:45.700 --> 00:10:49.660
The Financial Times has since written up Toyota's wider robotics push,

00:10:50.060 --> 00:10:51.700
including its Walden spinoff.

00:10:51.740 --> 00:10:56.120
Second, Teradyne Robotics, which owns Universal Robots,

00:10:56.120 --> 00:10:59.500
announced a partnership with Serect, a German startup.

00:10:59.500 --> 00:11:03.480
Serect Software runs Universal Robots arms that sort returned clothing for

00:11:03.480 --> 00:11:05.900
Zalando in Germany and Poland.

00:11:05.900 --> 00:11:08.500
One story is about scale and capital.

00:11:08.500 --> 00:11:11.380
The other is about who controls the software brain and

00:11:11.380 --> 00:11:13.780
who controls the route to customers.

00:11:13.780 --> 00:11:15.360
Together, they raise one question.

00:11:16.720 --> 00:11:20.640
If physical AI goes factory wide, who actually gets paid?

00:11:21.740 --> 00:11:25.780
Start with the Toyota number because the obvious calculation is misleading.

00:11:26.380 --> 00:11:27.240
That looks cheap.

00:11:27.800 --> 00:11:30.020
The catch is that Toyota gave an annual spend,

00:11:30.280 --> 00:11:32.140
but did not say how many years it would run.

00:11:32.700 --> 00:11:35.900
That falls within the same guide's range for a fully deployed system.

00:11:36.520 --> 00:11:37.300
Here is an analogy.

00:11:37.960 --> 00:11:39.760
Buying the arm is like buying the engine.

00:11:40.360 --> 00:11:42.900
You still need the gripper, the cameras and sensors,

00:11:43.420 --> 00:11:47.020
safety guarding, fixtures, integration engineering, and training.

00:11:47.020 --> 00:11:52.880
the same vendor guide says the arm is roughly 40 to 50 percent of the deployed cost

00:11:52.880 --> 00:11:59.220
integration and programming alone can take 25 to 35 percent that integration slice is where

00:11:59.220 --> 00:12:04.560
software companies like seric are aiming in a classic cell an integrator programs the robot

00:12:04.560 --> 00:12:10.360
for one known part in a fixed position change the part and you pay to program it again seric

00:12:10.360 --> 00:12:15.740
says its cortex model handles items it has never seen so you skip that per task engineering

00:12:15.740 --> 00:12:19.000
In its CEO, Ralph Golda's words,

00:12:19.420 --> 00:12:23.580
one brain serves every robot with no retraining for each new item or task.

00:12:24.220 --> 00:12:27.680
If that holds, the money moves away from integration hours and

00:12:27.680 --> 00:12:28.920
toward a software license.

00:12:29.500 --> 00:12:30.900
Returns are a hard test of it.

00:12:31.280 --> 00:12:34.520
A return jacket arrives crumpled in a bag at an odd angle,

00:12:34.900 --> 00:12:36.200
mixed with shoes and belts.

00:12:36.820 --> 00:12:39.700
Golda called returns one of the hardest jobs in logistics.

00:12:40.540 --> 00:12:43.360
The machine learning runs on NVIDIA hardware at the cell,

00:12:43.740 --> 00:12:45.700
trained in NVIDIA's simulation tool.

00:12:46.720 --> 00:12:49.680
So an engineer can trace the money in three parts.

00:12:50.160 --> 00:12:51.780
The ARM maker sells hardware.

00:12:52.400 --> 00:12:54.300
NVIDIA sells compute at the edge.

00:12:54.300 --> 00:12:59.720
The software company tries to replace the integrator's programming bill with a recurring fee.

00:13:01.320 --> 00:13:03.180
Some of this is established.

00:13:03.780 --> 00:13:06.900
Reuters reported Toyota's estimate from an investor discussion,

00:13:07.320 --> 00:13:09.200
and Assembly Magazine repeated it.

00:13:09.760 --> 00:13:12.940
Both say it includes humanoid and non-humanoid robots,

00:13:13.340 --> 00:13:15.300
plus replacements as well as new installs.

00:13:16.440 --> 00:13:21.180
Both also say Toyota has not committed to the spending or said how long it would last.

00:13:21.680 --> 00:13:22.620
That point matters.

00:13:23.140 --> 00:13:26.460
This is an estimate given to investors, not a capital budget.

00:13:27.020 --> 00:13:34.100
For scale, the International Federation of Robotics counted 542,000 industrial robots

00:13:34.100 --> 00:13:36.400
installed worldwide in 2024.

00:13:37.300 --> 00:13:40.260
Japan installed 44,500 of them.

00:13:40.900 --> 00:13:45.920
Even spread over several years, 400,000 robots for a single industrial group

00:13:45.920 --> 00:13:48.520
is a very large number against those figures.

00:13:49.280 --> 00:13:52.280
On the Sarecht side, the established facts are narrower.

00:13:53.720 --> 00:13:57.600
Teradyne and Sarecht say they have shipped hundreds of universal robots arms

00:13:57.600 --> 00:14:00.440
in dual-arm systems to live sites across Europe.

00:14:01.020 --> 00:14:06.620
They name Zalando, with CEVA Logistics as the operator, at Greven and Sweebedzin.

00:14:07.200 --> 00:14:10.060
They describe it as the first phase of a broader rollout.

00:14:10.260 --> 00:14:13.360
Every one of those claims comes from the companies themselves.

00:14:14.100 --> 00:14:19.620
Teradyne's commercial chief said the joint system has moved well beyond the demo or pilot stage.

00:14:20.440 --> 00:14:26.460
We have no independent site visit and no customer quote from Zalando or CEVA in the material.

00:14:27.220 --> 00:14:29.080
Several things are claimed but not shown.

00:14:29.660 --> 00:14:35.060
There are no pick rates, no exception rates, and no figures for how often a human steps in per shift.

00:14:35.760 --> 00:14:39.300
Nobody has published a cost per item processed or a payback period.

00:14:40.040 --> 00:14:44.440
The Nora training claim has no data on how performance holds when the item mix changes

00:14:44.440 --> 00:14:46.840
or when Cortex moves to a new site.

00:14:48.080 --> 00:14:53.120
On cost, our per-robot numbers come from a vendor's pricing guide, not audited data.

00:14:53.760 --> 00:14:59.740
Its claim that most cells pay back within 12 to 24 months in multi-shift work is a sales figure.

00:15:00.440 --> 00:15:02.680
The financial context is better sourced.

00:15:03.320 --> 00:15:08.840
Teradyne Robotics reported $91 million of revenue in the first quarter of 2026,

00:15:08.840 --> 00:15:10.800
after two rounds of layoffs.

00:15:12.380 --> 00:15:14.880
The same facts support different readings.

00:15:15.540 --> 00:15:19.740
The AGI forward reading says general purpose robot models are arriving now.

00:15:20.300 --> 00:15:23.980
One model that handles unseen items means automation costs

00:15:23.980 --> 00:15:26.100
stop growing with each new product,

00:15:26.560 --> 00:15:29.940
and Toyota's number signals that industrial buyers see it coming.

00:15:30.660 --> 00:15:33.860
On that view, the 400,000 figure is a floor.

00:15:34.520 --> 00:15:36.900
The AA Realist reading points to what is missing.

00:15:36.900 --> 00:15:43.740
Toyota's figure is an unsized, uncommitted estimate covering ordinary industrial robots,

00:15:44.300 --> 00:15:46.560
logistics automation, and humanoids together.

00:15:47.220 --> 00:15:51.820
Most of those 400,000 are probably conventional arms doing conventional work.

00:15:52.520 --> 00:15:55.700
Sarek's claims are self-reported, without exception rates.

00:15:56.360 --> 00:16:01.680
Reality usually bites in the exceptions, such as a sleeve caught in a gripper at 3 a.m.

00:16:01.680 --> 00:16:03.480
There is also some history.

00:16:03.480 --> 00:16:07.780
The FT-sourced reporting says investors remain cautious

00:16:07.780 --> 00:16:11.780
after Toyota fell short on hydrogen vehicles and solid-state batteries.

00:16:12.480 --> 00:16:15.280
The market reading is about power, not capability.

00:16:15.980 --> 00:16:18.600
Toyota buys at a scale that lets it dictate prices,

00:16:19.120 --> 00:16:21.140
and it has its own spinoff in Walden.

00:16:21.840 --> 00:16:26.220
Teradyne needs growth, so a partner like Sarict makes its arms more valuable.

00:16:26.880 --> 00:16:28.820
The policy reading is the most concrete.

00:16:28.820 --> 00:16:36.060
REIT. CERICT says its system helps retailers comply with European Union rules against destroying

00:16:36.060 --> 00:16:42.260
returned goods. That creates regulatory demand for returns processing, whatever the robotics

00:16:42.260 --> 00:16:50.060
hype cycle does. Our REIT is provisional. The Toyota number tells us demand is real and large,

00:16:50.060 --> 00:16:55.320
but it says almost nothing about margins for software vendors. A buyer at that scale will

00:16:55.320 --> 00:17:00.080
drive hardware toward commodity pricing, and it will want to own the model or buy from its own

00:17:00.080 --> 00:17:06.240
spinoff. The opening for a young company is a narrow, messy job like returns, where per-task

00:17:06.240 --> 00:17:12.900
integration has been too costly. Sarecht shows the playbook. It started with a named customer

00:17:12.900 --> 00:17:18.360
and a messy task, then used a hardware incumbent's installed base and integrator network for

00:17:18.360 --> 00:17:24.600
distribution. Teradyne claims more than 100,000 deployments behind that network. The risk sits

00:17:24.600 --> 00:17:28.820
on the other side of the same deal. The distributor owns the customer relationship,

00:17:29.400 --> 00:17:35.480
and NVIDIA owns the compute. Sarecht is not seed stage anymore, but a seed company copying its

00:17:35.480 --> 00:17:41.540
path would face the same squeeze with less leverage. One diligence question we would want

00:17:41.540 --> 00:17:47.660
answered is the cost per item handled, including human interventions, at the second and third sites

00:17:47.660 --> 00:17:52.960
rather than the first. We would also want to know who holds the contract, the software company,

00:17:52.960 --> 00:17:55.700
the arm maker, or the logistics operator.

00:17:56.340 --> 00:18:01.080
What would change our mind is a published exception rate that stays flat across sites.

00:18:02.780 --> 00:18:04.980
Three things would settle a lot of this.

00:18:05.500 --> 00:18:09.680
First, does Toyota turn the investor estimate into a committed capital plan,

00:18:10.040 --> 00:18:13.600
with a duration and a split between humanoid and conventional robots?

00:18:14.160 --> 00:18:16.460
Its next results briefings are the place to look.

00:18:17.120 --> 00:18:22.400
Second, do Zalando or CEVA publish throughput or cost figures for the returned cells,

00:18:22.400 --> 00:18:25.780
or extend the rollout past the first phase they describe.

00:18:26.420 --> 00:18:30.600
A named customer publishing numbers is worth more than any demo at a trade show.

00:18:31.880 --> 00:18:34.900
Third, does Teradyne Robotics revenue keep growing,

00:18:35.400 --> 00:18:38.260
and does it start to credit software partners like Sarict?

00:18:38.840 --> 00:18:40.880
If physical AI is moving hardware,

00:18:41.340 --> 00:18:45.480
it should show up in Teradyne's quarterly segment numbers over the next few quarters.

